@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "3. Put the player under your control") {
        Move the player while a direction is held and react once when Fire is pressed. Use game actions so touch, keyboard, and controllers can drive the same rules.

        Complete <doc:moving-with-systems-and-input> first. Start with the on-screen controls; hardware is optional.

        @Image(source: "spriteui-input-actions-02-preview.svg", alt: "A preview of the scene you will build in this lesson.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Name what the player can do") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            An action describes an intention, such as Move or Fire. A binding connects a control to that action. Your game can then ask for Move without knowing whether it came from a button or a stick.

            Move is a 2D value. Right contributes `[1, 0]` and Up contributes `[0, 1]`. Fire is a button action. The first stage shows these values before using them in a scene.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Run stage one. Hold Right and watch Move's x value. Release it and watch the value return to zero. Press and release Fire and compare its held state with its last event.

                Find the action definitions and virtual-control bindings. The buttons update one retained `InputController`; they do not move an entity directly.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-input-actions-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-input-actions-01-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                On a touch device, hold Up and Right together. The two controls combine into diagonal movement. A game rule will read the resulting vector, rather than handling two separate buttons.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Read movement from a system") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Held input is a value you read every frame. The movement system reads Move's vector, multiplies it by speed and `deltaTime`, and changes the player's position.

            The sample's `InputDemoDriver` component connects the player to the same controller the buttons use. It is a runtime connection, not saved game progress.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use stage two. Hold a direction: the cyan square moves until it reaches the edge. Release the button: it stops.

                Find the system's input read and compare it with the velocity calculation from the previous lesson. The source of direction has changed, but movement still belongs in the system.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-input-actions-02.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-input-actions-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-input-actions-02-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Change the speed and run again. The controls do not need to know how fast the player moves. They describe intent, and the game decides what that intent does.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "React once to a press") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Firing is different from walking. Walking continues while a control is held; a single shot should happen when the press begins. An input subscription observes Fire events and increments the counter only for `.pressed`.

            The final stage also uses `.gameInput` to connect keyboard, pointer, and gamepad controls. They feed the same Move and Fire actions as the on-screen buttons.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use the final stage. Hold Fire and check that the counter increases once. Release and press again for the next increment.

                If you have a keyboard, try WASD and Space. A supported controller can use its d-pad or left stick and south button. The virtual controls remain available.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-input-actions-03.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-input-actions-02.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-input-actions-03-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Hold a direction, disable input, and enable it again. Release before pressing again. This reset prevents a held control from becoming a surprise movement or shot after a menu closes.

                You can now distinguish held actions from one-time events. Continue with <doc:steering-and-collision-aware-movement> to add walls.
            }
        }
    }
}
